## Proof

We claim:

$$\mathrm{prob}(\min(X,Y)=k) = p_1 + p_2 - p_3.$$

**Key identity.** For every outcome $(x,y)$ in the sample space, the indicator of $\{\min(X,Y)=k\}$ plus the indicator of $\{\max(X,Y)=k\}$ equals the indicator of $\{X=k\}$ plus the indicator of $\{Y=k\}$:

$$\mathbf{1}[\min(x,y)=k] + \mathbf{1}[\max(x,y)=k] = \mathbf{1}[x=k] + \mathbf{1}[y=k]. \tag{$\ast$}$$

**Verification of ($\ast$).** Fix an outcome $(x,y)$ and examine all cases:

| Case | $\min=k$? | $\max=k$? | $X=k$? | $Y=k$? | LHS | RHS |
|------|-----------|-----------|--------|--------|-----|-----|
| $x=y=k$ | yes | yes | yes | yes | 2 | 2 |
| $x=k,\; y>k$ | yes | no | yes | no | 1 | 1 |
| $x=k,\; y<k$ | no | yes | yes | no | 1 | 1 |
| $x>k,\; y=k$ | yes | no | no | yes | 1 | 1 |
| $x<k,\; y=k$ | no | yes | no | yes | 1 | 1 |
| $x\neq k,\; y\neq k$ | no | no | no | no | 0 | 0 |

In every row the two sides agree, establishing ($\ast$). (The case analysis is exhaustive: either at least one of $x,y$ equals $k$ — giving the first five rows — or neither does — giving the last row.)

**Taking expectations.** Since $X,Y$ take finitely many integer values, the sample space is finite and ($\ast$) holds pointwise on every atom. Taking expectations of both sides:

$$\mathrm{prob}(\min(X,Y)=k) + \mathrm{prob}(\max(X,Y)=k) = \mathrm{prob}(X=k) + \mathrm{prob}(Y=k).$$

Substituting $p_1 = \mathrm{prob}(X=k)$, $p_2 = \mathrm{prob}(Y=k)$, $p_3 = \mathrm{prob}(\max(X,Y)=k)$ and solving:

$$\boxed{\mathrm{prob}(\min(X,Y)=k) = p_1 + p_2 - p_3.}$$

**Remark on independence.** The identity ($\ast$) is purely pointwise — it holds for every outcome regardless of the joint distribution. Hence no independence assumption is needed; the formula is valid for arbitrary (possibly dependent) $X$ and $Y$.

